{
 "text": "Cryptographers have a yardstick for this. Imagine an ideal hash: a gigantic ledger. Whenever a new input shows up, you flip five hundred and twelve fair coins and write the result down next to it. If the same input ever comes back, you look up what you wrote before. Perfectly unpredictable, and perfectly consistent.\n\nNo real function can be that ledger. But a good hash is supposed to be indistinguishable from it, for every practical purpose. The costs I quoted for the three games are what the ideal ledger would give you. When real hash functions have failed, it's because someone found a way to tell them apart from the ledger, a shortcut that made one of those games cheaper. That's what happened to the older functions M D five and SHA one, and we'll look at those stories in the episode on adversaries.",
 "text_sha256": "04ef56658d8c2918098754330f2cd69033858994b27f249b57edac0b9894fe39",
